---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gdpCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'c', label: 'consumption C', min: 20, max: 100, step: 1, value: 65 },
    { name: 'i', label: 'investment I', min: 5, max: 40, step: 1, value: 18 },
    { name: 'g', label: 'government G', min: 5, max: 60, step: 1, value: 20 },
    { name: 'nx', label: 'net exports (X − M)', min: -20, max: 20, step: 1, value: -3 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'GDP = C + I + G + NX', expr: 'c + i + g + nx' },
    { label: 'C share of GDP', expr: 'c/(c + i + g + nx)*100', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'trade contribution', expr: 'nx', unit: 'points' },
  ],
};

const gdpQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A bakery buys £200 of flour and sells £500 of bread. What enters GDP?',
      choices: [
        '£700 — both transactions count',
        '£500 — the final sale only; the flour is an intermediate good already embodied in the bread\'s price, and counting both would double-count',
        '£300 — the value added',
        '£0 — food is a necessity',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'GDP counts final goods to avoid double counting. The elegant equivalent: sum the value added at each stage (200 at the mill, 300 at the bakery) and you get 500 — the final price. Both methods agree by construction, which is why the output and expenditure approaches to national accounting must always tally.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which of these raises measured GDP?',
      choices: [
        'A parent caring for their own children',
        'Cleaning up after an oil spill, at a cost of £2 billion — the cleanup is a paid service, so it adds to GDP even though it only restores what was lost; the damage itself subtracted nothing',
        'The sale of a 2018 used car in 2026',
        'Volunteering at a charity shop',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'GDP measures marketed production, not welfare: it counts the repair and ignores the harm (and the unpaid work, and the depletion). This asymmetry — costs of cleanup in, damages out — is the broken-window problem at national scale, and the motive for green-adjusted accounts.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Nominal GDP rose 6% last year while the price level (deflator) rose 4%. Real GDP grew by:',
      choices: [
        '10%',
        'About 2% — real growth strips out inflation (approximately nominal minus inflation; exactly, (1.06/1.04) − 1 ≈ 1.9%)',
        '6% — the deflator is decorative',
        '−4%',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Nominal GDP mixes quantity with price; real GDP revalues output at constant prices so that "growth" means more goods and services, not dearer ones. Comparing economies, or an economy across decades, without deflating is comparing apples with dearer apples.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why do international comparisons use GDP per capita at purchasing power parity (PPP)?',
      choices: [
        'Because PPP is easier to calculate',
        'Because identical incomes buy different baskets in different countries — PPP converts to a common price level, so "what can you actually live on" is compared rather than the exchange-rate number',
        'Because currencies are unstable',
        'Because PPP counts unpaid work',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'A haircut that costs $40 in Oslo costs $4 in Delhi; converting incomes at market exchange rates understates living standards where prices are low. PPP adjustment is why India appears roughly a seventh of the US economy at market rates but over a third at PPP — both numbers are true, they answer different questions.',
    },
  ],
};

const gdpDefCloze = {
  "text": "GDP is the market value of all %%0%% goods and services produced in a country in a year. It is built from four spending streams: %%1%% + I + G + NX, where NX means %%2%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "final"
    ],
    [
      "C",
      "consumption"
    ],
    [
      "net exports",
      "exports minus imports"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Only final goods count, and the four buckets are consumption, investment, government and net exports."
};

const gdpLoafOrder = {
  "prompt": "A farmer, a miller and a baker make one loaf economy — put the value-added accounting in order:",
  "items": [
    "The farmer produces grain and sells it for £100 (value added £100)",
    "The miller buys the grain for £100 and sells flour for £150 (value added £50)",
    "The baker buys the flour for £150 and sells bread for £260 (value added £110)",
    "Total value added: £100 + £50 + £110 = £260",
    "The same £260 appears as household expenditure on the final bread"
  ],
  "why": "Each stage's value added is its output minus its intermediate inputs. Summing those gaps and summing final expenditure are the same arithmetic run in opposite directions."
};

const gdpCountSort = {
  "prompt": "Which of these enter this year's GDP?",
  "buckets": [
    "counts in GDP",
    "does not count"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "a new factory built this year",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "buying shares of an existing company",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a pension payment from the state",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a haircut bought at a salon",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a parent's unpaid childcare",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "the full resale price of a 2018 car",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "Only new final production counts. Share purchases transfer existing assets, pensions are transfers, unpaid work is invisible, and a resale adds only the dealer's margin."
};

const gdpBucketJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Consumption is typically 60–70% of GDP in rich economies.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Household spending is the giant bucket, which is why consumer confidence is macroeconomic news."
    },
    {
      "s": "Buying shares counts as investment in the national accounts.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Investment means new productive capacity — machines, factories, software, housing, inventories — not the transfer of existing assets."
    },
    {
      "s": "Transfer payments such as pensions are part of government spending G.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "They are excluded from G because they buy no output; their spending enters C when recipients spend them."
    }
  ]
};

const gdpRealEst = {
  "prompt": "Nominal GDP rose 6% while the price level rose 4%. What was real growth?",
  "unit": "%",
  "answer": 2,
  "factor": 1.1,
  "why": "Real growth strips out inflation: about 6% − 4% = 2%. The GDP deflator is the conversion factor (nominal ÷ real × 100).",
  "context": "every growth claim worth quoting is a real figure"
};

const gdpWelfareCompare = {
  "prompt": "One number, two questions — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "GDP",
    "text": "the market value of final output — built to track production through recessions, not to score a society"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "welfare dashboard",
    "text": "health, education, distribution, unpaid work and environmental depletion — what GDP leaves out"
  },
  "why": "Kuznets warned in 1934 that national income cannot measure a nation's welfare; the dashboard tries to count what the production total omits."
};

const gdpDoublePlace = {
  "prompt": "At 2% annual real growth, roughly how many years until output doubles? Place it on the axis.",
  "labels": [
    "0",
    "17.5",
    "35",
    "52.5",
    "70"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 70
  },
  "answer": 35,
  "tolerance": 1,
  "why": "The rule of 70 gives 70 ÷ 2 = 35 years. Compounding turns a small growth rate into a large change in living standards."
};

const gdpRecessionBranch = {
  "root": "Real GDP falls for two consecutive quarters, then grows in the third. Trace the diagnosis.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Using the conventional rule, what is this?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A recession — two consecutive quarterly falls",
          "next": "rule",
          "feedback": "Yes — the two falls meet the conventional threshold."
        },
        {
          "label": "Not a recession, because the third quarter grew",
          "next": "grew",
          "feedback": "The rule looks at the two consecutive falls; the later recovery does not erase them."
        },
        {
          "label": "A depression",
          "next": "depression",
          "feedback": "Depression is an informal, deeper and longer event; two quarters is the recession threshold."
        }
      ]
    },
    "rule": {
      "text": "The conventional rule is two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP. The US also uses the NBER's broader judgment — income, employment, sales and production, not GDP alone."
    },
    "grew": {
      "text": "The third quarter's growth marks the recovery, not the absence of the recession.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "So what is the rule?",
          "next": "rule",
          "feedback": "Two consecutive quarterly falls."
        }
      ]
    },
    "depression": {
      "text": "A depression is an informal label for a deeper, longer downturn; the conventional recession threshold is two quarters.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Review the threshold",
          "next": "rule",
          "feedback": "Two consecutive quarterly falls."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "why": "The two-quarter rule is a convention; the NBER's broader dating uses income, employment, sales and production because GDP alone can mislead."
};

const gdpLoafSpot = {
  "prompt": "Find the line where the value-added arithmetic goes wrong:",
  "lines": [
    "The farmer sells £100 of grain to the miller.",
    "The miller sells £150 of flour to the baker.",
    "The baker sells £260 of bread to households.",
    "GDP by value added is £100 + £150 + £260 = £510.",
    "The correct total is £260: £100 + £50 + £110."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Value added at each stage is £100, £50 and £110 — the £150 flour price and £260 bread price are not themselves value added.",
  "why": "Adding each stage's full selling price double-counts the intermediate goods already embodied in the final bread."
};

const gdpAlternativesMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each alternative measure with what it adds to GDP:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Human Development Index",
      "right": "averages income, life expectancy and schooling"
    },
    {
      "left": "Better Life Index",
      "right": "lets countries weight wellbeing dimensions"
    },
    {
      "left": "Gross National Happiness",
      "right": "Bhutan's dashboard of life satisfaction and environment"
    },
    {
      "left": "Green accounting",
      "right": "subtracts natural-capital depletion from saving"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Each alternative encodes a philosophy of what matters — and each is also an index-number choice with weighting politics."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/gdp-national-accounts">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Economics lesson. It builds on both foundational lessons:
    <em>Supply and Demand</em> (markets, prices and quantities are about to be added up
    across a whole economy) and <em>Money and Inflation</em> (the price level returns as
    the GDP deflator). No accounting background is assumed — the identities are derived
    from a single bakery on the page.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define GDP precisely and say why only final goods count;</li>
    <li>compute GDP by expenditure, income and output, and explain why the three must agree;</li>
    <li>read the four buckets (C, I, G, NX) and say what each includes and excludes;</li>
    <li>convert nominal to real, per capita and PPP figures, and match each to the question it answers;</li>
    <li>argue both sides of "GDP measures wellbeing" using Kuznets's own warning.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>GDP is the market value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a
    year</strong> — the most-watched number in economics. It is built from four spending streams:
    <strong>C + I + G + NX</strong> (consumption, investment, government, net exports). Knowing
    what each bucket holds — and what GDP deliberately ignores — is the difference between using
    the number and being used by it. GDP was invented for a specific job: Simon Kuznets built
    the first US national accounts for Congress in 1934, in the middle of the Great Depression,
    because policymakers discovered they were steering an economy they could not measure. The
    measurement worked so well that it colonised policy itself — "growth" became the master
    objective — which makes understanding what the number measures and what it leaves out a
    duty of every voter. This page covers the definition and the three ways to compute it, the
    bucket-by-bucket anatomy with real shares, nominal versus real and the deflator, what GDP
    counts and deliberately excludes, the criticisms that survive scrutiny, and the
    alternatives that try to measure what GDP does not.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="fill the blanks — the GDP definition" widget="cloze" config={gdpDefCloze} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="build an economy from its four spending streams — watch shares shift as you drag" widget="sliderLab" config={gdpCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag each of the four sliders and watch the GDP
    readout change by exactly the amount you moved — that one-for-one response is the
    identity C + I + G + NX in action. Then watch the share readouts: pushing consumption
    up squeezes every other bucket's share, and dragging net exports negative turns the
    trade contribution red before the total even flinches much.
  </p>

  <p>
    The readouts show typical rich-country shares: consumption ~60–70%, investment ~15–25%,
    government ~15–25%, trade hovering near zero (exports minus imports). Drag net exports very
    negative and you have a trade-deficit economy; drag government up and you have 1945 Britain.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="true or false — reading the four buckets" widget="judge" config={gdpBucketJudge} />

  <h2>One number, three routes</h2>
  <p>
    Every transaction in an economy has two sides — someone spends, someone earns, someone
    produces — so GDP can be measured three ways, and all three must give the same total.
    The <strong>expenditure approach</strong> sums spending on domestically produced final
    goods: <strong>GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)</strong>. The <strong>income approach</strong>
    sums what production pays out: wages, rents, interest, profits. The <strong>output
    approach</strong> sums the <em>value added</em> at every stage of production. The
    approaches agree by construction: the flour mill's £200 of value added plus the
    bakery's £300 equals the £500 final bread price — summing value added and summing
    final expenditure are the same arithmetic run in different directions. (In practice
    the three routes disagree slightly, and statistical agencies publish a
    <em>balancing</em> discrepancy — a rare, honest admission that measurement is hard.)
    The <strong>final-goods rule</strong> is what prevents double counting: flour sold to
    a bakery is an intermediate good and enters GDP only through the bread; but flour
    sold to a household to bake at home is final and counts directly. The dividing line
    is the buyer's use, not the good's nature.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="value added down the bread chain" widget="order" config={gdpLoafOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one number can be reached three ways — spending, income and
    value added — and the final-goods rule is what stops the same production being
    counted twice. Next: what actually sits inside each of the four spending buckets.
  </p>

  <h2>The four buckets, with their real weights</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Consumption (C)</strong> — household spending on goods and services — is the
    giant: roughly 60–70% of GDP in most rich economies (about 68% in the US, 60% in
    Germany), which is why "consumer confidence" is macroeconomic news. Within it,
    services (health, rent, education, streaming) dominate goods in rich economies —
    the sign of a mature economy. <strong>Investment (I)</strong> means something
    precise and narrower than the everyday word: business fixed investment (machines,
    factories, software), residential construction, and changes in inventories.
    Buying shares is <em>not</em> investment in this sense — it transfers ownership of
    existing assets; only new productive capacity counts. Investment is the smallest
    but most volatile bucket (15–25% of GDP, swinging hardest in recessions — the
    accelerator of the business cycle) and, uniquely, it builds the future's capacity.
    <strong>Government spending (G)</strong> — public services, defence, schools,
    infrastructure — is 15–25% in most developed economies (it peaks near 50%+ in
    wartime economies: 1945 Britain). The transfer payments (pensions, benefits)
    that dominate modern budgets are <em>excluded</em> from G — they are not
    purchases of output, and their spending enters C when recipients spend them.
    <strong>Net exports (X − M)</strong> nets sales abroad against purchases from
    abroad; it hovers near zero for large economies, runs persistently positive
    in Germany and China and deeply negative in the US and UK — and a negative
    number is not a "loss", just a subtraction from one accounting identity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="counts in GDP, or not?" widget="sort-buckets" config={gdpCountSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "buying shares is investment — I invested in the
  stock market." In the national accounts, investment means only new productive capacity:
  machines, factories, software, housing, inventories. Buying shares transfers ownership
  of existing assets from one pocket to another and adds nothing to production — which is
  why a booming stock market and a booming economy are different headlines.</p>

  <h2>Nominal, real, per capita, PPP: the transformations that matter</h2>
  <p>
    Four adjustments convert the raw total into comparable information.
    <strong>Nominal versus real:</strong> nominal GDP uses current prices, so it rises
    when prices rise as well as when output does. <strong>Real GDP</strong> revalues
    output at constant (base-year) prices, isolating quantity; the conversion factor
    is the <strong>GDP deflator</strong> (nominal ÷ real × 100). If nominal GDP rose
    6% while prices rose 4%, real growth was about 2% — and "growth" in every
    policy conversation means real growth. <strong>Per capita:</strong> dividing by
    population converts total to average — real GDP per capita is the standard
    (rough) proxy for living standards, and the number the growth-lessons'
    long-run charts plot. <strong>Purchasing power parity (PPP):</strong> market
    exchange rates compare economies at international prices, but identical
    incomes buy different baskets in different countries; PPP adjusts for local
    price levels, which is why India's economy looks roughly a seventh of the
    US at market rates but over a third at PPP. Each adjustment answers a
    different question — size, growth, typical income, affordable living — and
    quoting the wrong one for the question is the most common abuse of the
    statistic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the real growth rate" widget="estimate" config={gdpRealEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "nominal GDP rose 6% last year, so the economy grew
  6%." Nominal figures mix quantity with price: if prices rose 4% over the same year,
  real output grew only about 2%, and the rest of the headline was inflation. Every
  growth claim worth quoting is a real, and usually per capita, figure — the raw nominal
  number answers no useful question on its own.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the raw total has now been converted into comparable
    information — real for growth, per capita for typical income, PPP for affordable
    living — and each transformation answers a different question. Next: what the number
    never counted in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>What GDP deliberately excludes — and what that costs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Intermediate and second-hand transactions:</strong> flour inside bread, and the 2026 resale of a 2018 car (only the dealer's margin is new production). Financial trades likewise: buying shares transfers claims and adds nothing to production.</li>
    <li><strong>Unpaid work:</strong> a parent's childcare, volunteering, household cooking — enormous, real, and invisible. Estimates put unpaid household work at 20–50% of GDP if valued; notably, women's mass entry into paid work raised GDP partly by <em>re-categorising</em> work that was always being done.</li>
    <li><strong>The informal and illegal economies:</strong> cash-in-hand work, subsistence farming, smuggling. The shadow economy runs from a few percent of GDP (US, Switzerland) to a third or more in some developing economies, which makes poor countries look poorer than they live.</li>
    <li><strong>Distribution:</strong> GDP per capita is a mean, and means float above medians when income concentrates. A country's GDP can grow for a decade while the median household stagnates — the US 1979–2019 experience in miniature. Growth and its incidence are different questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Depreciation of natural and social capital:</strong> the oil extracted, the forest cleared, the burnout — depletion is production, not loss. An oil spill <em>raises</em> GDP (the cleanup is paid work) while the damage itself subtracted nothing: broken-window accounting, where destruction shows up as growth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Kuznets himself drew the line in his first report: <em>"the welfare of a nation
    can scarcely be inferred from a measurement of national income"</em> — a warning
    his successors' economies ignored for eighty years.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="production versus welfare — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={gdpWelfareCompare} />

  <h2>Growth, recessions and the business cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Real GDP's movements are the economy's vital signs. <strong>Economic growth</strong>
    is the long-run trend in real output — driven by labour, capital and
    productivity, and compounding: 2% annual growth doubles living standards in
    about 35 years (the rule of 70: 70 ÷ growth rate ≈ years to double). Around
    the trend runs the <strong>business cycle</strong>: a <strong>recession</strong> is
    conventionally two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP (the US also uses
    the NBER's broader judgment), with falling investment, rising unemployment
    and falling inflation typically in tow; <strong>recoveries</strong> climb back to
    trend. The quarterly GDP release moves markets, budgets and elections
    precisely because so many decisions key off it — one reason statistical
    agencies guard their independence jealously, and one reason GDP revisions
    (each quarter's figure is revised for years as better data arrives) make
    headlines that later quietly reverse.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="mark the doubling time at 2% growth" widget="place" config={gdpDoublePlace} />
  <Widget no="9" caption="is it a recession?" widget="branch" config={gdpRecessionBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "GDP measures how well a country is doing."
  It measures the volume of marketed production — a powerful, necessary
  instrument, and a poor welfare measure. Use it for what it was built for
  (tracking production through recessions, comparing economies over time),
  pair it with the transformations (real, per capita, PPP) and the
  distributional data it lacks, and treat single-number wellbeing claims
  — GDP up, country great — as the category error Kuznets named in 1934.</p>

  <h2>One loaf, three approaches, worked</h2>
  <p>
    The three routes agree by construction, but the agreement is worth
    watching once. A village economy in a day: a miller buys £100 of grain
    from a farmer and sells £150 of flour to a baker, who sells £260 of
    bread to households. <strong>Output approach:</strong> value added — farmer
    £100 (grain, assuming no inputs), miller £50, baker £110 — total
    <strong>£260</strong>. <strong>Expenditure approach:</strong> households' final
    purchases = <strong>£260</strong> (C). <strong>Income approach:</strong> every pound
    of value added is somebody's income — wages, rent, interest, profit —
    so the farmer's, miller's and baker's payouts also sum to
    <strong>£260</strong>. Nothing was counted twice, nothing was missed:
    the identity holds because these are three views of one set of
    transactions. Now complicate it — the baker also sells £40 of bread
    to a restaurant (an intermediate purchase, excluded from C but the
    restaurant's meals count later), the government buys £30 of bread
    for a school (G), a neighbouring village buys £20 (X) while the
    village imports £50 of cheese (M). The same three identities
    absorb all of it — and you can see why statistical agencies
    employ thousands of people and still publish discrepancies:
    the arithmetic is exact, the <em>measurement</em> of each leg
    is not.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the double-counted loaf" widget="spot-error" config={gdpLoafSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A bakery buys USD 200 of flour and sells USD 500 of bread. How much enters GDP?</li>
    <li>Nominal GDP rose 6% last year while the price level rose 4%. What was real growth?</li>
    <li>Cleaning up an oil spill raises GDP, while the spill itself subtracted nothing. What does this show about GDP as a measure?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) USD 500 — the final sale only; the flour is an
    intermediate good already embodied in the bread's price, and counting both would
    double-count. Equivalently, the value added at each stage (mill plus bakery) also
    sums to 500. (2) About 2% — real growth strips out inflation, since nominal figures
    rise when prices rise as well as when output does. (3) GDP measures marketed
    production, not welfare: the cleanup is paid work so it counts, but the damage was
    never subtracted — the broken-window asymmetry that motivates green-adjusted
    accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>GDP is the market value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a year.</li>
    <li>Three routes — expenditure, income, value added — must agree by construction; the final-goods rule prevents double counting.</li>
    <li>GDP = C + I + G + NX; investment means new capacity, never share purchases.</li>
    <li>Transfer payments enter G not at all — they become C when recipients spend them.</li>
    <li>Real, per capita and PPP conversions each answer a different question; quoting the wrong one is the commonest abuse.</li>
    <li>GDP excludes unpaid work, the informal economy, distribution and the depletion of natural capital.</li>
    <li>Recession is conventionally two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate shelf picks up the buckets one by one: <em>Fiscal Policy &amp;
    Public Debt</em> takes the G bucket and its deficits, <em>Monetary Policy &amp;
    Central Banks</em> steers the price level that separates real from nominal, and
    <em>Unemployment &amp; Labour Markets</em> follows the recessions this lesson
    defined. <em>Elasticity</em> returns to micro foundations to explain how quantities
    respond in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The alternatives, honestly compared.</strong> The <strong>Human Development
    Index</strong> (UN) averages income, life expectancy and schooling — a deliberate
    statement that health and education count; it reranks the world (Cuba, Sri
    Lanka score above their incomes). The <strong>Better Life Index</strong> (OECD),
    <strong>Gross National Happiness</strong> (Bhutan), and dashboards of wellbeing
    (life satisfaction, mental health, environment) each encode a philosophy of
    what matters; the honest critique of all of them is that they, too, are
    index-number choices with weighting politics. <strong>Green accounting</strong>
    (the World Bank's adjusted net savings, satellite environmental accounts)
    tries to subtract depletion directly; digital free goods (search, maps,
    messaging — massive consumer value, near-zero GDP) are the new frontier
    the accounts cannot price.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="pair each alternative with what it adds" widget="match" config={gdpAlternativesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How should national accounts value data and
    intangibles (software investment is now larger than machinery in several
    economies)? Does the platform economy's producer-surplus shift mean
    GDP undercounts welfare or overcounts it? And can a headline number
    ever be designed to resist being gamed by the government that
    publishes it? The GDP debate is, at bottom, the measurement
    ethics debate: every number that steers policy becomes a target,
    and every target has a shadow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The release culture, and why revisions matter.</strong> GDP arrives in
    the world imperfect on purpose: the first estimate of a quarter is
    published weeks after it ends, built on partial returns, and is then
    revised — twice formally, then again in annual rounds as tax, survey
    and business-register data complete. Revisions of half a percentage
    point are routine, and occasionally they redraw history (the 2008–09
    recession's depth was revised substantially deeper; Ireland's 2015
    "26% growth" was a corporate-tax artifact that the statisticians
    themselves flagged). The reader's craft matches the journalist's:
    treat first estimates as drafts, compare economies on the same
    vintage of data, and be most sceptical exactly when a
    headline number flatters the government of the week —
    the release calendar is a political instrument as
    well as a statistical one.
  </p>
